      "text": "You can solve the leg lengths with base-2 to be easy, but do you have to, or can you just work with the base-4 log function?\nThe easiest way would be to think about how you would raise a number to a power without using a calculator so I did not use a calculator with this problem. My attempt can be seen under the spoiler on the following page.\n$\\hbox{spoiler}$\n\nWe know that $\\log_{4}(27)=\\log_{4}(3^3) = \\frac{3}{2} \\log_{4}3$ and $\\log_{4}(9)=\\log_{4}(3^2)=\\frac{2}{2} \\log_{4}3$. So, $\\log_4 27+\\log_4 9=\\frac{5}{2}\\log_4 3$. Additionally, we evaluate $(4^{\\frac{1}{2}})^3 \\cdot (4^{\\frac{2}{2}})^2=4^{(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{2}{2})} = 4^3=64$ so that $\\log_4 3=\\frac{1}{2}$. Replacing back into the previous proof, we get that we have that $\\log_4 27 =\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{4}$ and that $\\log_4 9 = \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}$, so that the hypotenuse is equal to \\[ \\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{4})^2 + (\\frac{1}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3+\\frac{1}{2}}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\] Finally, \\[4^h = 4^{\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}} = 2^{\\sqrt{7}}\\] and \\[2^{\\sqrt{7}} = 2^2 \\cdot 2^{\\sqrt{7} - 2} = 4 \\cdot 2^{\\sqrt{7} - 2}\\] or \\[\\boxed{4 \\cdot 2^{\\sqrt{7} - 2}}\\quad 4^h = 4 \\cdot 2^{\\sqrt{7} - 2}\\]",
